{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass in the morning.</b> The reason for his using an expression of distress is to tell of the anguish that this righteous man suffered over Rachel being withheld from him." "<b>And behold, it was Leah.</b> The reason it is written with a vav is to allude to the words of the people of the place, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 70:12), who were murmuring during the night and saying ״She is Leah,״ and in the morning he recognized that utterance which the people of the place had aroused him to — for it was indeed Leah. Also, close examination of the word ״behold״ is well founded." "<b>What is this you have done, etc.</b> One must understand his intent in complaining ״What is this,״ for if it was about the deception, behold he says ״and why did you deceive me״ — this tells that in addition to the deception, something else reprehensible had preceded it." "<b>Indeed,</b> Jacob was complaining about two things: the first, about the act itself — that he gave him Leah in place of Rachel; and the second, that he did the thing by way of deception. And he reveals his mind in this, that he would not have been so insistent had he not <b>done</b> this deception, and it would have passed over him in silence. And it was worse and more wicked because of the deception he perpetrated against him. And the reason for his vehemence is on account of what resulted for him through this error, that he intended Rachel and it was Leah, and improper things resulted from this, as I explained at the verse ״וַיְהִי בָעֶרֶב״ (Genesis 29:23), and the righteous are most particular about what touches upon spirituality." "<b>Further,</b> he intends by saying <b>and why did you deceive me</b>, that there is in this a disgrace for the one who sits in his bosom, that she should be married by way of deception, and this would diminish the cherished bond of the union. And since he had firmly decided in his mind that she would surely be his wife forever, he was particular about this." "<b>Further,</b> he spoke wisely with these words, seeing that what was done was done and there is no remedy for what had been performed, for it is not the way of proper conduct to demand a reversal. And he agreed in his mind to take Rachel as well; therefore he did not press hard on the distinction between Leah and Rachel, for he feared that Laban would increase the bridal service for Rachel yet more, and say to him: let us make a new arrangement, and want to take more than seven years. Therefore he said, <b>וְלָמָּה רִמִּיתָנִי</b> — meaning, why did you do the thing by way of deception, for you could have reconciled me to Leah without employing deception, since they are all equally for good."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:03.167780247Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}